--- (2) ASSERTION AUDIT — do the names actually assert the property? ---

replay-c2uqki-sweep-noop.test.mjs:995-1020 (the clause-1/2 test, verbatim):

  // ── (5) FR-D1.7 — a fix filed mid-run blocks the open verifiers ────────────
  describe("FIX_BEFORE_VERIFY=enforce — no verifier runs against unfixed code", () => {
    it("QA does not start until TEAM-4241 completes, and CI not until TEAM-4242 does", async () => {
      await loadWith({ sweep: "off", fixBefore: "enforce" });
      await deliver(doneRecord(ANALYST));
      await deliver(doneRecord(DETECT));

      // 12:30:13 — the reviewer files its fix while QA and CI are still open.
      await deliver(insertFixRecord(REVIEW_FIX));
      const afterReviewFix = h.state.blockerWrites.filter((w) => w.ids.includes(REVIEW_FIX));
      expect(afterReviewFix.map((w) => w.ticketId).sort()).toEqual([QA, CI].sort());
      expect(h.state.board.get(QA).blockedBy).toContain(REVIEW_FIX);

      await deliver(doneRecord(REVIEW));
      await deliver(doneRecord(REVIEW_FIX));

      // 12:53:27 — QA files its own fix, 1m42s before closing its own ticket, so
      // BOTH open verifiers are held: CI has not run yet, and QA's own re-verify
      // round must not start against the unfixed ledger either.
      await deliver(insertFixRecord(QA_FIX));
      expect(h.state.blockerWrites.filter((w) => w.ids.includes(QA_FIX)).map((w) => w.ticketId).sort())
        .toEqual([QA, CI].sort());
      expect(h.state.board.get(CI).blockedBy).toContain(QA_FIX);
    });
  });

ANALYSIS of the quoted assertions:
  * Clause 1 (QA held until TEAM-4241 completes) — ASSERTED, via blocker edge:
      expect(h.state.board.get(QA).blockedBy).toContain(REVIEW_FIX);
    where REVIEW_FIX is TEAM-4241. PROVEN.
  * Clause 2 (CI held until TEAM-4242 completes) — ASSERTED, via blocker edge:
      expect(h.state.board.get(CI).blockedBy).toContain(QA_FIX);
    where QA_FIX is TEAM-4242. PROVEN.
  * Both are actually STRICTER than the acceptance wording. The acceptance text
    pairs one fix with one verifier; the code blocks BOTH verifiers on EACH fix:
      expect(afterReviewFix.map((w) => w.ticketId).sort()).toEqual([QA, CI].sort());
      expect(...w.ids.includes(QA_FIX)...).toEqual([QA, CI].sort());
    Deviation in the safe direction, but it IS a deviation: TEAM-4241 also blocks
    CI, and TEAM-4242 also blocks QA's own re-verify round.

  EVIDENCE GAP (edge != dispatch): the test asserts the blocker EDGE is written.
  It does not assert the held verifier is then not INVOKED, even though this
  harness captures every dispatch (h.state.invokes, dispatchedTo(), line 585).
  Dispatch suppression is covered only indirectly, by fix-before-verify.test.mjs
  'blocks each open verifier exactly once, preserving a mid-flight status'.

